  "summary": "Traditional reception room design and furnishing style for hosting guests in Arab homes and public spaces.",
  "description": "Majlis interiors feature low seating arrangements around the perimeter with cushions and carpets, integrated ventilation systems like wind towers or mashrabiya windows, and decorative elements showcasing Arabic calligraphy and geometric patterns. This space reflects Bedouin hospitality traditions and Islamic social customs, with layouts designed to encourage conversation while maintaining gender separation in traditional settings.",
  "culturalContext": "The majlis — literally \"place of sitting\" — is the reception heart of Arab and wider Islamic social life, from Bedouin tents to Ottoman-era Damascene houses to Gulf palaces, where a ruler or elder receives petitioners, hosts poets and settles disputes. UNESCO inscribed the majlis as living heritage in 2015; contemporary Gulf majlises still stage weekly audiences beside qahwa service, keeping governance, hospitality and architecture fused.",
